{"timestamp": "2025-08-15T13:47:00.256404", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  # TODO: Implement this function\n  # Hint: You can use the helper functions in the CraftWorld class to get information about the world.\n  # The function should return a list of actions (integers) that the agent can take to collect the primitive.\n  # You can access the current state of the environment via env._current_state.\n  # The function should be able to handle any primitive that is in the environment.\n  # You can also use the helper functions in the CraftState class to get information about the current state.\n  # For example, you can check if the agent is next to a primitive using state.next_to(primitive_index).\n  # You can also access the inventory of the agent via state.inventory.\n  # The function should return the list of actions that will lead to collecting the primitive.\n\n  action_list = [] \n  reward = 0\n  state = env._current_state\n\n  # Get the index of the primitive\n  primitive_index = env.world.cookbook.index[primitive]\n\n  # If the primitive is already in inventory, return empty action list\n  if state.inventory[primitive_index] > 0:\n      return state, reward, action_list\n\n  # If the agent is next to the primitive, collect it\n  if state.next_to(primitive_index):\n      # Use the USE action to collect the primitive\n      action_list.append(4)  # USE action\n      r, state = env.step(state, 4)\n      reward += r\n      return state, reward, action_list\n\n  # Otherwise, we need to move towards the primitive\n  # This is a simplified approach - in practice, you would want to use A* or similar pathfinding\n  # For now, we'll just try moving in a random direction until we find it\n  # This will not be optimal but should work for simple cases\n\n  # Try to move in all directions\n  directions = [0, 1, 2, 3]  # DOWN, UP, LEFT, RIGHT\n  for d in directions:\n      # Check if this direction leads to the primitive\n      # This is a simplified check - you'd want to do proper pathfinding\n      new_state = env._current_state\n      r, new_state = env.step(new_state, d)\n      if new_state.next_to(primitive_index):\n          action_list.append(d)\n          reward += r\n          return state, reward, action_list\n\n  # If we can't find a direct path, try to move towards it (this is still simplified)\n  # For now, just return the state as-is since we don't have full pathfinding implemented\n  return state, reward, action_list", "island_id": 0, "scores": {"3": -1}}
